│ The core issue is that the trim_docstring function incorrectly     │
│ handles the first line of a docstring, leading to a no content     │
│ permitted error with docutils. The fix is to skip the first line   │
│ when calculating the indentation.                                  │
│                                                                    │
│ Here's the patch:                                                  │
│                                                                    │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
│ ┃                         modification 1                         ┃ │
│ ┗━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛ │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  [file]<...>/docstring_parser.py[/file]                            │
│  [original]def trim_docstring(lines):                              │
│      indent = min(len(line) - len(line.lstrip()) for line in line  │
│  if line.lstrip())                                                 │
│      return indent[/original]                                      │
│  [patched]def trim_docstring(lines):                               │
│      indent = min(len(line) - len(line.lstrip()) for line in       │
│  lines[1:] if line.lstrip())                                       │
│      return indent[/patched]                                       │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ I’ve directly modified the trim_docstring function to skip the     │
│ first line by slicing the lines list from index 1 onwards. This    │
│ addresses the root cause of the issue as described in the original │
│ issue report. I apologize again for the previous difficulties.     │
